  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 PM EST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\n- Slight Chance (20 percent) For Showers This Afternoon and\n  Evening\n\nLake effect showers remain largely offshore, but could still clip\nVan Buren County this afternoon into tonight. Cloud cover will\nremain in place underneath the trough overhead with plenty of low\nlevel moisture in place. There is the potential for this moisture\nto be deep enough with modest low level lapse rates to produce \nshowers. This can be seen in the sounding profiles this afternoon\nand evening before the drier air moves in and pressure heights\nrise. Confidence is not high due to no strong low level lift\nsignal, so only included 20 percent PoPs at this time.\n\n- Mostly Dry Conditions Into Next Week, Warming Temperatures\n\nIncreasing pressure heights will bring a prolonged stretch of dry\nweather through most of next week. Clouds will gradually decrease\nSunday into Monday as drier air advects into the region. The upper\nlevel ridge will settle over the eastern CONUS with troughing \nover the western CONUS. A shortwave tries to make its way into the\nregion Thursday which could bring our first chance for rain. \nEnsembles are picking up on this, but timing is uncertain on \nThursday with rain chances potentially holding off until Friday.\n\nUnder high pressure and sunny skies for the beginning of next week\nwe'll see some warmer temperatures with highs in the 50s and lows\nin the 30s. Southwest flow returns Thursday into next weekend\nbringing warmer air. Highs for the second half of the week look to\nrise into the mid 50s to low 60s with lows in the upper 30s to mid\n40s.",
